Appeal from Final and Interlocutory Judgments
Statutes and Federal Rules—and in particular, 28 U.S.C. §§ 1291–1292 and Rule 54(a)–(b)—determine when a court judgment is appealable. Most centrally, Section 1291 gives the courts of appeals jurisdiction over appeals from “final decisions.” This creates the crucial distinction between final decisions of district courts, which are appealable, and non-final, or interlocutory, decisions, which generally are not.

28 U.S.C. § 1291
Final Decisions of District Courts

The courts of appeals (other than the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit) shall have jurisdiction of appeals from all final decisions of the district courts of the United States, * * * except where a direct review may be had in the Supreme Court. * * *



•Courts of appeals shall have jurisdiction over appeals from “final decisions” of the district courts

NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON THE FINAL JUDGMENT RULE AND 28 U.S.C. § 1291
1. What does it take for a court order or judgment to be “final”? The basic idea is this: “A ‘final decision’ generally is one which ends the litigation on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but execute the judgment.” Catlin v. United States, 324 U.S. 229, 233 (1945). This is known as the “final judgment rule.” Note that the final judgment rule does not limit final judgments only to decisions that end the litigation in every respect. Rather, the final judgment rule limits final decisions to those decisions that end litigation on the merits. Thus, litigation not on the merits may remain. Further, the final judgment rule does not limit final judgments to decisions that leave the court nothing to do.
